<text x="170.0" y="58" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#8b949e" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:0.70s">a·b = Σ aᵢbᵢ</text>
<text x="170.0" y="82" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#8b949e" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:1.05s">a·b = |a||b| cos θ</text>
<text x="170.0" y="106" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#8b949e" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:1.40s">cos θ = similarity score</text>
<text x="170.0" y="130" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#8b949e" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:1.75s">θ = 0° → identical direction</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. il12-math-linalg-D1 — </strong>The dot product measures how much two vectors point the same way. Dividing by the lengths gives cosine similarity — the score behind search, recommendations, and embeddings.</figcaption></figure>

Worked example: A=[[1, 2], [3, 4]], B=[[5, 6], [7, 8]]. C[i][j] = Σ_k A[i][k]·B[k][j]. C[0][0] = 1×5+2×7 = **19**; C[0][1] = 1×6+2×8 = **22**; C[1][0] = 3×5+4×7 = **43**; C[1][1] = 3×6+4×8 = **50**. So AB = **[[19, 22], [43, 50]]**. Note BA differs: BA = [[23, 34], [31, 46]] — order matters. A matrix is a linear map; multiplication is composition of maps.

