<text x="170.0" y="58" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#8b949e" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:0.70s">Solve det(A − λI) = 0</text>
<text x="170.0" y="82" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#8b949e" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:1.05s">λ from: λ² − tr(A)λ + det(A) = 0</text>
<text x="170.0" y="106" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#8b949e" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:1.40s">tr(A) = λ₁ + λ₂</text>
<text x="170.0" y="130" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#8b949e" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:1.75s">det(A) = λ₁ · λ₂</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. il12-math-linalg-D3 — </strong>Eigenvalues are the stretch factors along the directions a matrix leaves unchanged. For a 2×2 they fall out of one quadratic whose coefficients are the trace and determinant — a built-in checksum.</figcaption></figure>

Fit y = mx through points (1,2), (2,3), (3,5): minimize Σ(mxᵢ − yᵢ)². Normal equation: m = Σxᵢyᵢ/Σxᵢ². Σxᵢyᵢ = 1×2+2×3+3×5 = **23**; Σxᵢ² = 1+4+9 = **14**; m = 23/14 ≈ **1.6429**. Residuals: predictions 1.64, 3.29, 4.93 vs actual 2, 3, 5 — the best single slope in the least-squares sense. This is linear regression with one parameter; the full version solves (XᵀX)⁻¹Xᵀy. Neural networks are linear algebra plus nonlinearity: start here.

