<text x="606.0" y="84" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="14" fill="#f0f6fc" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="700" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:3.50s">β̂</text>
<text x="606.0" y="104" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#8b949e" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:3.85s">best-fit line</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. il12-math-linalg-D4 — </strong>Least squares finds the line (or hyperplane) minimizing the sum of squared residuals. Squaring penalizes large misses hardest, which is also why a single outlier can drag the whole fit.</figcaption></figure>

Worked example on latency samples (ms): [12, 15, 18, 22, 22, 27, 31, 35, 40, 48]. Mean = Σx/n = 270/10 = **27**. Median (middle value) = **24.5**. Population std dev = **10.91**. Mean − median gap signals skew: the 48 drags the mean above the median. Rule: report center AND spread together; a mean without a spread is half a fact. Training, evaluation, and uncertainty are statistics.

