Ordered selections: P(n,k) = n!/(n−k)!. Unordered: C(n,k) = n!/(k!(n−k)!). Worked example: choose a 3-person committee from 10 people. C(10,3) = 10!/(3!·7!) = **120**. If the 3 roles are distinct (ordered), P(10,3) = **720**. Sanity check: C(n,k) = C(n,n−k); here C(10,3) = C(10,7) = 120 — both computed, both equal. Ask 'does order matter?' first: committees no, passwords yes. Counting and induction for algorithm analysis in the ML modules.

