The object model vocabulary. ML libraries are built from these ideas — scikit-learn's estimator API is classes-with-contracts all the way down.
- **Class vs instance** — A class is the blueprint; an instance is the object. Attributes set in __init__ define the object's state; methods define its behavior.
- **Dunder methods** — Double-underscore hooks (__init__, __repr__, __len__, __eq__, __iter__) that let your objects speak Python's native protocols. __repr__ is the single highest-ROI dunder: it makes debugging possible.
- **Inheritance vs composition** — Inheritance says 'is-a' (a Retriever is a Component); composition says 'has-a' (a Pipeline has a Retriever and a Reranker). Composition is the default in modern codebases because it avoids fragile base-class coupling.
- **Module vs package** — A module is one .py file; a package is a directory of modules with __init__.py. Imports resolve through sys.path — which is why 'run from the project root' is a rule, not a suggestion.
- **Virtual environment** — An isolated Python installation (venv) with its own packages. One project, one environment: the only sane way to keep dependency versions from fighting.
- **pip and requirements** — pip installs packages; a pinned requirements file (package==version) makes the environment reproducible. 'Works on my machine' dies here.
- **Type hints** — Annotations (def f(x: int) -> str) documenting expected types. They are not enforced at runtime by default, but they turn editors and type checkers into bug catchers.
- **Docstring** — The string literal at the top of a module, class, or function documenting what it does. help() and documentation tools read it; future-you reads it more.
- **Estimator contract** — The ML idiom this module points at: objects exposing fit/predict (or transform) with get_params/set_params. Learn it here and scikit-learn will feel familiar on sight.
- **__name__ == '__main__'** — The guard that lets a file be both an importable module and a runnable script. Every CLI entry point in this course uses it.

