Concurrency vocabulary with the GIL front and center. Get these straight and you'll never again reach for threads to speed up a training loop.
- **GIL (Global Interpreter Lock)** — CPython's lock allowing only one thread to execute Python bytecode at a time. It is released during I/O waits and in C extensions like NumPy — which is exactly why threads help downloads but not pure-Python number crunching.
- **I/O-bound vs CPU-bound** — I/O-bound work waits on the network or disk (threads help: the GIL is released while waiting). CPU-bound work burns Python bytecode (threads don't help: the GIL serializes them). Classify first, parallelize second.
- **threading** — OS threads sharing one process and one memory space. Cheap to start, shared state is easy — and shared state is where the bugs live.
- **multiprocessing** — Separate processes, each with its own GIL and memory. True CPU parallelism in Python, at the cost of inter-process communication overhead and higher memory use.
- **concurrent.futures** — The high-level interface: ThreadPoolExecutor and ProcessPoolExecutor with a uniform submit/map API. The right default for most parallel work — reach for raw threads/processes only when you outgrow it.
- **Race condition** — Two threads interleaving operations on shared state so the result depends on timing. The classic: two threads incrementing one counter and losing updates.
- **Queue** — A thread-safe FIFO (queue.Queue) for passing work between threads. The standard pattern: producers feed the queue, worker threads consume it — no manual locking on the handoff.
- **Lock** — A mutual-exclusion primitive: only the lock holder runs the critical section. Use sparingly — a design needing many locks usually wants a queue instead.
- **asyncio (context)** — Single-threaded cooperative concurrency with async/await. Powerful for massive I/O fan-out; out of scope for this module but the name you'll meet next.
- **Embarrassingly parallel** — Work that splits into independent chunks (map a function over files). If your problem isn't this, parallel speedup math gets ugly fast.

