**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *The GIL serializes CPU-bound threads; I/O-bound work and multiprocessing are unaffected.* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.

**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on.

