Array vocabulary. These terms recur verbatim in PyTorch, JAX-style frameworks, and every data pipeline you'll build.
- **ndarray** — NumPy's n-dimensional array: a fixed-type, contiguous block of numbers with a shape. The fundamental data structure of numerical Python.
- **Shape** — The tuple describing each axis length, e.g. (1000, 784) reads as '1000 samples, 784 features'. Shape errors are the most common bug in ML code — read shapes first, debug second.
- **dtype** — The element type (float32, int64, ...). It controls memory footprint and numeric behavior; mixing dtypes silently upcasts, sometimes expensively.
- **Broadcasting** — NumPy's rules for operating on mismatched shapes: dimensions are aligned from the right, size-1 axes stretch. Powerful, and the source of silent shape bugs when misapplied.
- **Vectorization** — Replacing explicit Python loops with array operations executed in compiled C. The performance difference is routinely 10-100x — the single most important optimization habit in data code.
- **Boolean mask** — An array of True/False used to select elements (arr[arr > 0]). The idiomatic way to filter data without loops.
- **View vs copy** — Slicing returns a view (shared memory); fancy indexing returns a copy. Mutating a view mutates the original — know which one you hold.
- **Axis** — The dimension an operation reduces or acts along. axis=0 vs axis=1 confusion is a rite of passage; draw the array and point at the axis.
- **Seed** — The starting state of a pseudorandom generator. Setting it makes 'random' splits, shuffles, and initializations reproducible across runs.
- **Stride (intuition)** — How many memory steps separate consecutive elements along an axis. You rarely set strides directly, but the concept explains why some operations are fast and transposes are nearly free.

