The wrangling vocabulary. Every data pipeline you'll ever build is composed from these operations.
- **DataFrame / Series** — pandas' 2D table and 1D column. The DataFrame is the working surface of tabular data science; the Series is a single typed column with an index.
- **Index** — The row labels aligning every column. Merges, joins, and assignments all align on indexes — silent misalignment is the classic pandas bug.
- **loc vs iloc** — Label-based vs position-based selection. Mixing them up (especially with integer indexes) is a top-three pandas error; be explicit every time.
- **groupby** — Split-apply-combine: partition rows by key, aggregate each group, reassemble. The engine of 'per-customer', 'per-day', 'per-category' analysis.
- **Merge / join** — Combining tables on keys: inner, left, right, outer. Each join type answers a different question about which rows survive — and row counts must be checked after every merge.
- **Tidy data** — One observation per row, one variable per column. Messy wide tables (years as columns, values in headers) must be melted before analysis.
- **Missing-data mechanisms** — MCAR/MAR/MNAR in one line: missing randomly, missing predictably, missing because of the value itself. The mechanism constrains which fixes are honest.
- **Chained assignment** — df[a][b] = v may write to a temporary copy and silently do nothing. One .loc call, one assignment — the SettingWithCopyWarning is telling the truth.
- **Vectorized string/datetime accessors** — .str and .dt apply operations to whole columns without loops. The pandas answer to 'for each row, parse the date'.
- **Categorical dtype** — Memory-efficient storage for low-cardinality text with defined ordering. Also the correct way to tell a model which categories exist, including unseen ones.

