The statistical vocabulary behind every evaluation table you'll read. Precision here prevents self-deception later.
- **Distribution** — The shape of how values occur: normal, skewed, heavy-tailed, bimodal. The mean is a trustworthy summary only when you know the shape.
- **Central limit theorem (intuition)** — Averages of many independent samples tend toward normal, even when the underlying data isn't. This is why means of large samples are well-behaved — and why it says nothing about small samples.
- **Sampling bias** — When the collected sample systematically differs from the population of interest. No modeling technique fixes a biased sample; it can only launder it.
- **Confidence interval** — A range computed from sample data that, under repeated sampling, would contain the true value at the stated rate (e.g. 95%). It is not 'a 95% chance the truth is in this interval'.
- **p-value (plainly)** — The probability of seeing data this extreme if the null hypothesis were true. Small p: the null looks implausible. It is not the probability the hypothesis is true.
- **Multiple comparisons** — Testing many hypotheses guarantees some false 'discoveries'. The more tests you run, the more skepticism each positive deserves.
- **Bias-variance tradeoff** — Simple models underfit (high bias); complex models overfit (high variance). Error decomposes into bias, variance, and irreducible noise — the map for 'more data or more capacity?'.
- **Overfitting (statistical view)** — Fitting noise as if it were signal. Training error keeps falling while true error rises — the signature the validation split exists to catch.
- **Base rate** — The background prevalence of the thing you're detecting. Ignoring it makes even accurate tests misleading on rare events — the classic '99% accurate' fallacy.
- **Effect size** — How big the difference is, separate from whether it's statistically significant. With enough data, trivial differences become 'significant' — significance without size is noise.

