Workflow vocabulary. These are habits, not algorithms — and habits are what survive contact with real data.
- **Train / validation / test** — Train fits, validation selects (hyperparameters, early stopping), test estimates final performance — used once. Touching test during development is peeking at the exam.
- **Stratification** — Preserving class ratios in each split. On imbalanced data, unstratified splits can put all rare cases in one fold and lie to you.
- **Data leakage** — Information from outside the training set (or from the future) reaching the model. The #1 cause of 'amazing' results that evaporate in production.
- **Pipeline (preprocessing)** — Bundling preprocessing with the model so transformations fit on train folds only. The structural fix for the most common leakage vector: scaling/imputing on the full dataset.
- **Reproducibility** — Same code + same data + same seed + same environment = same result. Version everything; 'it worked yesterday' is not a result.
- **Baseline** — The simplest reasonable model (majority class, linear model, heuristic). Every complex model must beat it to earn its complexity — most don't, and that's information.
- **Experiment tracking** — Logging parameters, metrics, and artifacts per run. Memory is not a tracking system; the log is what lets you compare runs honestly.
- **Notebook → script** — Exploratory notebooks are for thinking; scripts are for running. The conversion forces ordering, removes hidden state, and makes the work schedulable.
- **Hidden state (notebooks)** — Out-of-order cell execution creating results the saved notebook can't reproduce. Restart-and-run-all is the honesty check.
- **Validation strategy** — The choice of how to estimate generalization: holdout, k-fold, time-based splits for temporal data. Random splits on time-ordered data are leakage.

