The supervised vocabulary. Everything in this path — tuning, metrics, features — plugs into these ideas.
- **Supervised learning** — Learning a mapping from inputs X to labeled targets y. Regression predicts numbers; classification predicts categories. Labels are the supervision.
- **Estimator API** — The shared contract: fit(X, y) learns, predict(X) predicts, transform(X) converts. Learn it once and every library feels familiar.
- **Linear / logistic regression** — Linear models: weighted sums, with logistic regression squeezing the sum through a sigmoid for classification. Strong baselines, interpretable coefficients, and the right default until data says otherwise.
- **Decision trees** — Recursive splits on feature thresholds, chosen to reduce impurity. Interpretable and nonlinearity-friendly — but unstable alone, which is why forests exist.
- **Random forests** — Many trees on bootstrapped samples, voting or averaging. Variance reduction through bagging: the reliable workhorse for tabular data.
- **Overfitting** — The model memorizing training noise. Signature: training score keeps improving while validation stalls or drops.
- **Regularization** — Penalties on complexity (L1/L2 on weights, tree depth limits, min-samples splits). The dial between underfit and overfit.
- **Support vectors / margin (intuition)** — SVMs find the boundary with maximum margin; only the nearest points (support vectors) define it. Kernels implicitly map to higher dimensions for nonlinear boundaries.
- **Inductive bias** — What a model family assumes before seeing data (linearity, smoothness, tree structure). Choosing a model is choosing a bias — match it to the problem.
- **No free lunch (practical)** — No model wins everywhere. The honest workflow tries several families with proper validation and lets the data decide.

