<text x="386.0" y="124" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="12" fill="#f0f6fc" text-anchor="start" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:3.15s">• Slower, parameter-sensitive</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. unsupervised-learning-D1 — </strong>K-means assumes spherical clusters and a known k; DBSCAN finds arbitrary shapes and labels noise but needs density tuning. No ground truth means no accuracy score — validate clusters by stability and business sense.</figcaption></figure>

Cluster real data and practice the skepticism: every result gets challenged before it's believed.
1. **k-means by hand, then by library.** Implement two iterations of k-means manually on 2D data to feel the centroid update; then use the library version. Compare — the library just does your loop fast.
2. **Choose k three ways.** Elbow plot, silhouette scores, and domain reasoning on the same dataset. When they disagree, write which you'd trust and why — there is no ground truth to appeal to.
3. **DBSCAN the hard case.** Cluster data with non-spherical shapes (moons/circles) where k-means fails visibly. Tune epsilon and min-samples; observe noise labels as a feature, not a bug.
4. **Read a dendrogram.** Hierarchical clustering on a small dataset; cut the tree at two different heights and compare the resulting groupings. State what each cut claims about the data.
5. **PCA honestly.** Reduce a high-D dataset to 2D; report variance explained; plot it. Then show a case where PCA's 2D view hides structure a nonlinear method reveals — variance is not importance.
6. **Validate externally.** Take your clusters and check them against something outside the clustering: held-out labels if they exist, stability across subsamples, or a downstream task. A cluster story with no external check is a hypothesis, labeled as such.

