Validation vocabulary. The theme: your score is only as honest as the process that produced it.
- **k-fold cross-validation** — Splitting data into k folds, training on k-1 and validating on the held-out fold, rotating. The mean estimates performance; the spread estimates its stability.
- **Stratified k-fold** — Each fold preserves the overall class distribution. On imbalanced data, plain k-fold can produce folds missing rare classes entirely.
- **Grid search** — Exhaustively trying every combination in a parameter grid. Thorough and exponentially expensive — fine for small spaces, hopeless for large ones.
- **Randomized search** — Sampling parameter combinations randomly. Surprisingly competitive: it explores more distinct values of the important parameters per unit budget.
- **Pipeline-inside-fold** — Preprocessing (scaling, imputation, encoding) fitted on each training fold separately. The structural guarantee against preprocessing leakage.
- **Nested CV** — An outer loop for unbiased performance estimation around an inner loop for tuning. Required when you tune and report on the same data — at roughly k×k the cost.
- **Validation vs test, again** — Tuning on validation, reporting on test. Tuning on test is the same peeking sin at higher resolution.
- **The tuning budget** — Every configuration tried is a multiple comparison. Huge searches overfit the validation set — keep a truly held-out test for the final word.
- **Early stopping as tuning** — Training duration is itself a hyperparameter; stopping on validation performance is tuning, and it belongs inside the validation scheme.
- **Reproducibility of searches** — Seeds for the search sampler and the CV splitter, logged configs. A 'best' config you can't reproduce is trivia.

