Feature vocabulary. The audit mindset matters more than any single transformation.
- **Feature engineering** — Creating model-ready inputs from raw data: encodings, interactions, aggregations, extractions. Where domain knowledge becomes model performance.
- **One-hot encoding** — A binary column per category. Simple and model-neutral; explodes dimensionality on high-cardinality features.
- **Ordinal / target encoding** — Numbers for ordered categories; category means for high-cardinality ones. Target encoding is powerful and leak-prone — it must be computed inside CV folds.
- **Scaling (standardization/normalization)** — Centering/scaling features. Essential for distance- and gradient-based models; irrelevant for trees. Fit on train only — the leakage rule again.
- **Feature selection** — Filter (correlation, mutual information), wrapper (recursive elimination), embedded (L1, tree importance). Fewer, better features beat more noisy ones.
- **Datetime features** — Hour, weekday, month, elapsed times, cyclical encodings (sin/cos for hour-of-day). Time is almost always signal — extract it deliberately.
- **Text features** — Lengths, counts, TF-IDF, embeddings. Start simple: character/word counts often carry surprising signal before any NLP.
- **Interaction features** — Products or ratios of features capturing combined effects. Powerful and combinatorial — validate that they add signal, not just columns.
- **Leakage audit** — The checklist: could this feature exist at prediction time? Was it computed with future info? Does it separate the target suspiciously well? Kill on suspicion.
- **Train-serve skew** — Features computed differently in training vs serving. The pipeline must compute them identically — the feature store exists for this reason.

