<text x="386.0" y="124" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="12" fill="#f0f6fc" text-anchor="start" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:3.15s">• Robust: median/IQR vs outliers</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. feature-engineering-D2 — </strong>Scale features for distance- and gradient-based models: standardization for regularized/NN models, robust scaling when outliers lurk. Tree models don&#x27;t care — skip scaling there and spend the effort on encodings.</figcaption></figure>

Engineer features on a real dataset and audit them like an adversary. Every feature must earn its place.
1. **Profile the raw features.** Types, cardinalities, missingness, and a first look at target relationships. Write the feature inventory before transforming anything.
2. **Encode categoricals three ways.** One-hot, ordinal, and target encoding (inside CV folds!) on the same feature; compare model impact. Feel the dimensionality and leakage tradeoffs directly.
3. **Scale and observe interactions.** Standardize features; show which models improve (kNN, SVM, regularized linear) and which don't care (trees). The 'which models need scaling' rule, demonstrated.
4. **Build datetime and text features.** Extract hour/weekday/cyclical features from timestamps; add text length and token-count features. Measure the validation lift of each addition — keep only what pays.
5. **Select ruthlessly.** Apply a selection method; compare full vs reduced feature sets on validation. Fewer features, same score: take the simpler set.
6. **Run the leakage audit.** For every feature, answer: available at prediction time? Computed without future info? Suspiciously predictive? Remove or fix the failures and re-measure — the score drop is the truth arriving.

