Regularization vocabulary. Every technique here buys generalization at the price of training fit — that's the trade, stated plainly.
- **Dropout** — Randomly zeroing activations during training, forcing redundant representations. An ensemble approximation; disabled at inference (with scaling). Helps most on large fully-connected layers.
- **Early stopping** — Halting training when validation performance stops improving (with patience). The cheapest regularization you own — and it requires a validation set you don't tune on.
- **Weight decay (L2)** — Penalizing large weights in the loss. Keeps weights small and diffuse; in AdamW it's decoupled from the gradient for cleaner behavior.
- **Normalization layers** — BatchNorm/LayerNorm: normalizing activations to stabilize training. They smooth the optimization landscape and permit higher learning rates.
- **BatchNorm vs LayerNorm** — BatchNorm normalizes across the batch (batch-size sensitive); LayerNorm across features (batch-independent — the transformer/norm default).
- **Data augmentation as regularization** — More (virtual) data constrains the model toward invariances. Often the highest-ROI regularizer when applicable.
- **Patience** — How many non-improving epochs you wait before stopping. Too little: stops into noise. Too much: wastes compute. A meta-hyperparameter worth setting deliberately.
- **Systematic tuning** — Changing one variable at a time against a fixed validation protocol. Alchemy is changing five things and declaring victory.
- **The held-out rule** — The set you stop on is not the set you report on. Early stopping consumes validation signal; final numbers come from untouched test data.
- **Underfitting vs overfitting (deep)** — Both still happen: too little capacity/regularization imbalance shows in the train/val gap. Read the gap before reaching for techniques.

