Transformer vocabulary. The attention operation is the whole game — everything else is scaffolding.
- **Self-attention** — Each position computes queries, keys, and values; attention weights (softmax of QK^T) mix the values. Every token directly consults every other token — long-range dependencies with no recurrence.
- **Queries, keys, values (QKV)** — The retrieval metaphor: queries ask, keys advertise, values carry content. Attention is differentiable dictionary lookup over the sequence.
- **Multi-head attention** — Several attention operations in parallel subspaces, concatenated. Different heads learn different relation types (syntax, coreference, position).
- **Positional encoding** — Attention is permutation-invariant, so order must be injected: sinusoidal patterns or learned/rope embeddings added to token representations.
- **Encoder vs decoder-only** — Encoders read bidirectionally (understanding tasks); decoder-only models predict next tokens with causal masking (generation). The field converged on decoder-only for LLMs.
- **Causal masking** — Hiding future positions during training so next-token prediction is honest. The mask is what makes autoregressive training valid.
- **Parallelism advantage** — Attention computes all positions simultaneously during training; recurrence is inherently sequential. This is why transformers scaled and RNNs hit a wall.
- **Quadratic cost** — Attention memory/compute grows with sequence length squared. The price of all-to-all consultation — and the motive behind every efficient-attention variant.
- **Scaling (honest)** — Larger models + more data + more compute → better capabilities, predictably-ish. But: costs grow faster than capabilities, data quality beats raw size, and some abilities emerge unevenly.
- **Residual connections + LayerNorm** — The scaffolding making deep transformers trainable: skip connections preserve gradient flow, normalization stabilizes it. Architecture details that decide whether training works at all.

