<text x="170.0" y="130" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#8b949e" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:1.75s">Inference: cache KVs, decode step by step</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. gpt-vs-bert-D2 — </strong>Causal masking hides future positions so training can process all tokens in parallel while preserving autoregressive order. At inference, KV-caching avoids recomputing past keys and values — the latency optimization behind fast generation.</figcaption></figure>

Compare the two objectives directly. Small models, visible differences, honest conclusions.
1. **Train tiny both ways.** On a toy corpus, train a small autoregressive model and a small masked model. Compare what each does well on completion vs fill-in-the-blank probes.
2. **Probe bidirectionality.** Give both models a sentence with an ambiguous middle word; show the masked model using right-side context the autoregressive model can't see. The architectural fork, demonstrated.
3. **Fine-tune the encoder.** Add a classification head to a small BERT-style model; fine-tune on a tiny labeled set. The classic 2019 workflow, reproduced.
4. **Prompt the generator.** Solve the same classification task by prompting a generative model (no weight updates). Compare data efficiency: labels vs prompt engineering.
5. **Break generation.** Ask the masked model to generate fluent text; watch it struggle. Then ask the autoregressive model for embeddings; note the awkwardness. Each family outside its home turf.
6. **Write the obituary-or-not.** Argue in writing where encoder models still beat decoder-only in 2026 (retrieval, embedding, efficient classification) — the takeover was economic, not total.

