Generate deliberately and evaluate honestly. The exercises build taste and skepticism in equal measure.
1. **Denoise a toy signal.** Implement 1D diffusion on a toy distribution: noise it forward, learn the reverse, sample. The whole idea in twenty lines — noise to signal, observed.
2. **Sweep the dials.** With a text-to-image tool, fix a prompt and sweep steps and guidance scale; grid the results. Write the quality/speed/fidelity tradeoffs you observe — the practitioner's calibration.
3. **Condition deliberately.** Take one subject through five style conditionings; note what the text encoder captures well (style, composition) and poorly (counting, text rendering, hands — the classic failure list).
4. **Edit, don't just generate.** Inpaint an object out of an image; outpaint a border. Compare the control this gives versus pure generation — editing is the production use case.
5. **Stress video honestly.** Generate short clips with motion; catalog the failure modes (morphing, physics violations, temporal inconsistency). Write the as-of capability statement: what it's good for now.
6. **Run the evidence test.** Produce a photorealistic image of a plausible-but-false scene; then write the disclosure and provenance notes it would need. The honesty exercise: capability without truth.

