Applied-perception vocabulary. The theme: perception is plumbing — data in, structure out, latency budgeted.
- **Object detection** — Boxes + labels for things in images. Evaluated with IoU-based mAP — the metric with its own learning curve, worth understanding before trusting a number.
- **Segmentation** — Per-pixel labels (semantic) or per-object masks (instance). Masks for precise boundaries; boxes when location suffices — match the output to the need.
- **OCR / document intelligence** — Pixels to structured data: text detection, recognition, layout analysis, key-value extraction. The pipeline with the most production deployments in applied vision.
- **Speech recognition (ASR)** — Audio to text: feature extraction, acoustic modeling, language modeling in modern end-to-end form. Word error rate (WER) is the scoreboard.
- **Speech synthesis (TTS)** — Text to audio: intelligibility vs naturalness, voice cloning's capabilities and consent requirements. The ethics are part of the engineering.
- **Preprocessing decides quality** — Denoising, VAD, resampling for audio; deskewing, binarization for documents. Garbage in is literal here — the model can't recover what preprocessing destroyed.
- **Streaming / real-time constraints** — Audio/video can't wait: chunk sizes, lookahead, and end-to-end latency budgets in the tens-to-hundreds of milliseconds. Architecture follows the budget.
- **VAD (voice activity detection)** — Finding speech in audio streams. The unglamorous gatekeeper of every voice pipeline — and a common failure point.
- **Latency budgets** — Decomposing the millisecond allowance across capture, preprocess, inference, and postprocess. If the sum exceeds the budget, something gets smaller, faster, or dumber.
- **Multimodal apps** — Vision/speech feeding LLMs: describe-then-reason, tool-call the perceiver, stream the results. Integration patterns, not magic.

