Representation vocabulary. The ladder from strings to meaning, one rung at a time.
- **Tokenization** — Splitting text into model-consumable units. The granularity decision (word/subword/char) shapes vocabulary size, sequence length, and OOV behavior.
- **Subword tokenization** — Splitting rare words into frequent pieces ('unhappiness' → 'un' + 'happiness'). The modern standard: open vocabulary with bounded size.
- **BPE / WordPiece** — Byte-Pair Encoding and WordPiece: merge-based vocabularies learned from data by repeatedly joining frequent pairs. Intuition over implementation.
- **Vocabulary size tradeoff** — Bigger vocab: shorter sequences, more parameters, rarer tokens. Smaller: longer sequences, better sharing. The standard ~30-50k is a compromise.
- **Distributional hypothesis** — 'You shall know a word by the company it keeps.' Words in similar contexts get similar vectors — the idea behind all static embeddings.
- **Word2Vec / GloVe** — Prediction-based (skip-gram/CBOW) and count-based (co-occurrence factorization) static embeddings. One vector per word — brilliant, and superseded for most tasks.
- **Static vs contextual** — Static: one vector per word type ('bank' = one vector). Contextual: vectors depend on the sentence ('river bank' ≠ 'bank account'). Context won because meaning is contextual.
- **OOV (out-of-vocabulary)** — Words unseen in training. Subwords compose them from pieces — the practical end of the OOV problem for most languages.
- **Embedding geometry** — Similar meanings cluster; analogies ('king - man + woman') work approximately. Geometry reflects the training objective — inspect, don't worship.
- **Special tokens** — [CLS], [SEP], <pad>, <unk>, <bos>/<eos>: the control vocabulary around real tokens. Know what each does in your model's format.

