Run the classical pipeline end to end and debug it stage by stage — the inspectability is the lesson.
1. **Tag and chunk.** POS-tag sample text; chunk noun phrases; inspect every decision. When the tagger errs, trace why — the debuggability classical methods offer.
2. **Recognize entities.** Run classical NER; evaluate precision/recall/F1 on a labeled sample. Examine the false positives — feature-based errors are explainable errors.
3. **Glance at parses.** Parse a few sentences (constituency and dependency views); identify where each representation helps a downstream task. Parsing's purpose, not its algorithms.
4. **Engineer features.** Build a tiny classifier on hand-engineered text features (word shapes, affixes, gazetteer hits); compare against a no-feature baseline. The classical craft, practiced.
5. **Win with a baseline.** On a small well-defined task, beat (or nearly match) a heavy model with a classical pipeline — and document the latency/interpretability advantages. The 'baselines that win' demo.
6. **Trace the cascade.** Inject a tagging error deliberately; watch it propagate through chunking to NER. Stage-by-stage debuggability vs end-to-end opacity — the tradeoff, observed.

