Tool-use vocabulary. The contract mindset: the model is a caller, your tools are the API.
- **Function calling** — The model emitting structured calls (name + arguments) instead of text. The bridge from reasoning to acting — schemas make it reliable.
- **Tool schema** — The JSON description: name, parameters, types, descriptions. The model only knows what the schema tells it — write schemas like documentation.
- **Tool as contract** — Inputs, outputs, error modes, and side effects specified. Vague tools get misused; contracted tools get composed.
- **Structured output** — Constraining tool arguments and results to schemas. Deterministic parsing — the difference between a demo and a system.
- **Idempotency** — Safe-to-retry operations. Tool calls fail and get retried; non-idempotent tools (send email, charge card) need extra guards.
- **Error handling** — Tool failures are normal: timeouts, bad args, downstream errors. Retries with backoff, fallbacks, and honest failure messages — not silent swallowing.
- **Least privilege** — Each tool exposes the minimum capability its job needs. A search tool doesn't get delete rights — the security half of tool design.
- **Tool allowlists** — Explicit sets of permitted tools per agent/task. The agent can't call what isn't listed — the first access-control layer.
- **Human-in-the-loop gates** — Confirmation steps before irreversible actions. The pattern for send/publish/delete: propose, confirm, execute.
- **Prompt injection via tools** — Tool outputs are untrusted text; malicious content can steer the model. Treat tool results as data, never as instructions.

