Protocol vocabulary. Standards are agreements that reduce integration work — understand what MCP standardizes.
- **MCP thesis** — One open protocol between AI applications and tools/data/workflows, replacing bespoke per-integration wiring. Write once, connect anywhere the protocol is spoken.
- **Host / client / server** — Host: the AI application. Client: the protocol participant inside the host. Server: exposes capabilities. Know who calls whom — the topology question.
- **Tools primitive** — Executable functions the model can call. The 'act' side of the protocol.
- **Resources primitive** — Addressable data the model can read (files, records, documents). The 'perceive' side — data with URIs, not just tool outputs.
- **Prompts primitive** — Reusable prompt templates servers can offer. Standardizing the 'how to ask' alongside the 'what to use'.
- **Discovery and routing** — Clients discovering server capabilities and routing requests. The dynamic composition that makes the protocol more than a spec.
- **Authorization boundaries** — Who may invoke what: per-server, per-tool, per-resource grants. The protocol doesn't remove auth — it needs it explicitly.
- **A2A (agent-to-agent)** — Agent-to-agent messaging as the complementary layer: MCP connects agents to tools/data; A2A connects agents to each other. Different layers, different jobs.
- **When MCP wins** — Many tools, many hosts, or an ecosystem play: the standard amortizes. One bespoke integration doesn't need a protocol.
- **Trust boundaries** — Servers are trust domains: validate inputs, scope capabilities, audit calls. A protocol standardizes the wire, not the trust.

