<text x="170.0" y="82" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#8b949e" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:1.05s">Resources: app-loaded context</text>
<text x="170.0" y="106" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#8b949e" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:1.40s">Prompts: reusable templates</text>
<text x="170.0" y="130" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#8b949e" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:1.75s">Servers choose what to expose</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. mcp-protocol-D2 — </strong>MCP servers expose three primitives: tools the model can call, resources the app can load as context, prompts as reusable templates. Design servers around the smallest useful capability set — every exposed tool is attack surface.</figcaption></figure>

Build on the protocol for real. The exercises go from using servers to writing one.
1. **Use an MCP server.** Connect a host to an existing server; list its tools/resources/prompts. Feel the discovery: capabilities arriving over the protocol, not compiled in.
2. **Compare the wiring.** Integrate the same tool two ways: bespoke function-calling vs MCP server. Count the integration code each way — the thesis, measured in lines.
3. **Build a server.** Expose a small dataset (resources) and two tools via MCP: discovery, routing, and error handling. Your server, speaking the standard.
4. **Scope the trust.** Apply the security checklist to your server: authorization per tool, least-privilege capabilities, allowlisted operations. Write the threat model in one page.
5. **Route A2A vs MCP.** Design a two-agent task: decide what goes over MCP (tools/data) vs A2A (agent messaging). The layer decision, defended in writing.
6. **Break the trust.** Attempt an over-privileged call against your server; verify the boundary holds. Then document what the protocol does and doesn't guarantee.

