Context vocabulary. The discipline: smallest high-signal token set that does the job.
- **Context engineering vs prompt engineering** — Prompting crafts instructions; context engineering curates everything the model sees: instructions, history, tool results, retrieved facts. The broader discipline.
- **Context rot** — Performance degrading as context grows: the model loses the thread in long inputs. Bigger windows don't fix focus — curation does.
- **Attention budget** — The model's finite capacity to weigh relevant tokens. Every token competes; low-signal tokens tax the budget. Spend it deliberately.
- **Compaction** — Summarizing long transcripts into dense state: decisions, open items, key facts. Minimal performance degradation is the bar — measure it.
- **Tool-result clearing** — Dropping bulky tool payloads after use while keeping the call record (what was called, what it returned in brief). Re-fetchable beats retained.
- **Structured memory** — Notes, facts, and plans stored outside the window in queryable form. The window holds the now; memory holds the known.
- **Just-in-time retrieval** — Fetching memory into context only when relevant, not preloading everything. The memory analog of lazy loading.
- **System-prompt altitude** — What belongs in the persistent system prompt (roles, durable rules) vs the working context (task state). Altitude errors waste the budget.
- **The small-context discipline** — Actively minimizing tokens in play: the cheapest, most reliable performance intervention for agents. Measure tokens like latency.
- **Evaluation of context strategies** — A/B compaction and clearing strategies on task success, not vibes. Context design is empirical.

