Low-code agent vocabulary. The power is speed; the obligation is governance.
- **Topics** — Conversation subjects with trigger phrases routing user intents. The low-code dialog model: intents in, flows out.
- **Trigger phrases** — Example utterances mapping to topics. Coverage and disambiguation are the design work — the NLU you configure, not code.
- **Actions** — Steps the agent takes: API calls, flows, connectors. The 'act' side, configured not coded.
- **Connectors** — Prebuilt integrations to business systems (SharePoint, Dataverse, web services). The fastest path from agent to real data — and the permission surface.
- **Generative answers** — LLM responses grounded in specified knowledge sources, with citations. RAG, productized: your data answering through the agent.
- **Knowledge sources** — The documents/sites the agent may draw on. Curation is governance: the agent knows what you let it read.
- **Publishing / sharing** — Deploying to channels (Teams, web) and audiences. Distribution is easy — which is why the governance must come first.
- **DLP (data loss prevention)** — Policies controlling what data agents can touch and exfiltrate. The guardrail between a helpful agent and a leak.
- **Access control** — Agents acting with user vs bot identity; respecting source permissions. The agent must not see what the user can't.
- **The governance gate** — No production data before DLP + access review. Afternoon prototypes are fine; production needs the gate.

