Exam-vocabulary ML. Recognition and fit — the exam's two moves.
- **Supervised (exam framing)** — Learning from labeled data: regression and classification. The exam's definition first, implementation never.
- **Unsupervised (exam framing)** — Finding structure without labels: clustering. Know the one-line distinction cold.
- **Deep learning (exam framing)** — Neural networks with many layers, for images/speech/complex patterns. The exam associates it with the right workloads.
- **Azure Machine Learning workspace** — The organizational container: experiments, models, compute, data. The studio's unit of organization.
- **Compute targets** — Where training runs: managed compute options. The exam wants 'training needs compute' matched to the concept.
- **Datastores / datasets** — Registered data references for experiments. Data versioning as a concept, not a tutorial.
- **AutoML** — Automated model selection and tuning: upload data, get candidate models. The no-code path — know what it automates and what it doesn't.
- **Designer** — Drag-and-drop ML pipelines. Visual composition of the classic workflow: data → train → score → evaluate.
- **Training vs validation (exam)** — Train fits, validation tunes/selects. The vocabulary the exam's questions assume — same discipline as the ML path, in Microsoft's words.
- **No-code fit** — When AutoML/designer suffice (standard tabular problems, quick baselines) vs when code is needed (custom architectures, production pipelines).

