Monitoring vocabulary. Deployed models rot — the question is whether you notice.
- **Data drift** — Input distribution shifting (feature values move). The model sees unfamiliar territory — performance may follow.
- **Concept drift** — The relationship itself changing (P(y|X) moves): what used to predict churn doesn't. Retraining on new labels is the fix; new features may be needed.
- **The distinction that matters** — Data drift: inputs moved. Concept drift: the world changed. Different diagnoses, different fixes — conflating them wastes retraining.
- **Reference windows** — The baseline period current data is compared against. Chosen deliberately: too short is noisy, too long hides change.
- **KS / PSI / chi-square** — Distribution-comparison tests: Kolmogorov-Smirnov (continuous), Population Stability Index (binned), chi-square (categorical). The instruments, with their sensitivities.
- **Prediction drift** — The output distribution shifting before labels arrive. The early warning: predictions moving means something moved — investigate.
- **Retraining triggers** — Schedules (periodic), thresholds (drift detected), pipelines (automated). The policy answering 'when do we retrain' before the incident.
- **ML metadata / lineage** — Which data, code, and parameters produced this model. The audit trail making 'which model saw what' answerable.
- **Silent decay** — Performance eroding with no errors, no alerts, no crashes. The characteristic production ML failure — monitoring exists for this.
- **Incident playbook** — The runbook for decay: detection signals, diagnosis steps, rollback criteria, retraining procedure, communication. Written before the incident.

