AgentOps vocabulary. Traces are the unit of understanding for non-deterministic systems.
- **Why tracing, not just metrics** — LLM runs are non-deterministic with branching tool calls and chains; aggregates hide the story. Traces preserve the actual execution — the debuggable unit.
- **Trace anatomy** — Spans (individual operations: model calls, tool calls, retrievals), sessions (grouped interactions), and multi-turn conversations as inspectable records. Learn to read one like a log.
- **Spans and hierarchy** — Nested spans showing the call tree: agent → model → tool → retrieval. The structure revealing where time, tokens, and failures concentrate.
- **Production dashboards** — Cost, latency, and quality tracked across live traffic: per-span breakdowns, error rates, user feedback. The operational view.
- **Trace-to-dataset** — Failing runs become the evaluation set: export traces, label outcomes, iterate against them. Production failures as training data for your evals.
- **User annotations** — Thumbs up/down and corrections captured in production. The cheapest labeled data you'll ever get — if you instrument for it.
- **Online evaluators** — LLM-as-judge or rule checks scoring live traffic: groundedness, safety, task completion. Monitors built from the same primitives as evals.
- **Automated failure detection** — Patterns over traces: loops, error cascades, cost spikes, quality drops. Alerting on behavior, not just infrastructure.
- **OpenTelemetry-native** — Standard tracing protocols avoiding vendor lock-in: instrument once, export anywhere. The self-hosting answer.
- **Cost attribution** — Per-trace, per-span, per-user token accounting. The bill, explained — and the data behind cost guardrails.

