<text x="306" y="140" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#3fb950" text-anchor="end" font-weight="600" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:4.55s">excessive agency</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. prod-red-team-D2 — </strong>Four attack classes: jailbreaks bypass policy, prompt injection turns untrusted content into instructions, tools exfiltrate data, excess agency gets abused. Test all four — attackers won&#x27;t limit themselves to your favorite.</figcaption></figure>

Attack your own agent, then defend it. Every defense gets tested by the attack it claims to stop.
1. **Checklist the threats.** Map the OWASP LLM Top 10 against your agent from the agentic path: which apply, with the concrete attack path per item. The threat model, written.
2. **Inject it.** Direct prompt injection against your agent; then indirect (malicious document content). Document what worked — the attacker's view, honestly recorded.
3. **Break the output.** Get the agent to emit something dangerous if mishandled (script tags, SQL); show the insecure-handling consequence. Then validate/sanitize outputs — the fix, demonstrated.
4. **Over-privilege it.** Give the agent excessive agency deliberately; demonstrate the blast radius. Then apply least privilege and re-test — the radius, shrunk.
5. **Burn the budget.** Craft inputs maximizing cost (long loops, huge contexts); measure the spend. Then implement rate limits and cost caps — denial-of-wallet, defended.
6. **Harness it.** Build the eval harness: 30+ adversarial cases, judges, and release gates. Run it against your hardened agent; every gate must pass with the evidence logged.

