Evaluate real agent runs three ways. The triangulation is the lesson.
1. **Collect trajectories.** Run your agent on 20 tasks; save full trajectories (thoughts, calls, observations). The raw material — everything after needs it.
2. **Component-score them.** Code checks per run: right tools? valid args? under step budget? The cheap deterministic layer — and note what it can't see.
3. **Judge them.** LLM-as-judge on rubrics (correctness, efficiency); then spot-check 5 against your own judgment. Find where the judge disagrees with you — that's its bias, documented.
4. **Human-calibrate.** Annotate 10 trajectories yourself; measure judge-vs-human agreement. The calibration telling you when the judge is trustworthy.
5. **Golden them.** Curate 10 excellent trajectories as the regression set; deliberately regress the agent (worse prompt) and watch the set catch it. The anti-regression asset, proven.
6. **Experiment once.** Change one variable (the system prompt); run the eval set before/after; claim the gain only if the numbers move. Then promote the evaluator to a production monitor sketch.

