Feature-platform vocabulary. Consistency between training and serving is the product.
- **Feature-store thesis** — One canonical feature definition serving both training and inference. Define once, compute consistently everywhere — the skew killer.
- **Offline store** — Historical feature values for training backfill: point-in-time correct datasets at scale. The training side.
- **Online store** — Low-latency key-value serving of latest feature values. The inference side — milliseconds matter here.
- **Point-in-time correctness** — Training rows joined to feature values as of the event time, not as of now. The mechanism preventing future leakage at scale.
- **Features as code** — Entities, feature views, and transformations defined in versioned code via SDK. Reviewable, testable, reproducible — like the rest of the stack.
- **Entity** — The subject of features (user, item, session): the join key organizing the store. Get entities wrong and everything downstream wobbles.
- **Feature view** — A logical group of features with their freshness and source definitions. The unit of organization and reuse.
- **Training/serving skew (revisited)** — The store's reason for existing: without it, training and serving compute features differently and models degrade silently.
- **When it earns it** — Many features, many models, real-time serving, strict consistency needs. A handful of batch features doesn't need a platform.
- **Platform boundaries** — Feature store (features) vs model registry (models) vs data versioning (raw data): three systems, three jobs, drawn honestly without overlap wars.

