Edge vocabulary. The constraints are physical — respect them or the device will.
- **Edge thesis** — Latency (no round trip), privacy (data stays local), offline (no connectivity): the three reasons inference leaves the cloud.
- **ONNX** — Open Neural Network Exchange: the interchange format. Export once, run across frameworks and runtimes — the portability layer.
- **Quantization for edge** — 8-bit down to sub-4-bit: smaller models, faster on-device inference, lower power. Quality tradeoffs first — measured per task.
- **On-device runtimes** — ONNX Runtime and friends with execution providers (CPU, mobile NPUs, accelerators). The runtime is half the performance story.
- **Execution providers** — Pluggable backends mapping ops to hardware accelerators. Right provider, right chip — or the model runs on the slow path.
- **GGUF / local LLMs** — Quantized weight formats and C++ inference engines for CPU/Apple-silicon/GPU local serving. LLMs without the cloud — with real limits.
- **Memory budgets** — Weights + activations + runtime must fit device RAM. The first constraint that kills edge deployments — budget it first.
- **Battery and thermal** — Sustained inference drains batteries and throttles chips. Burst benchmarks lie; sustained performance is the truth.
- **Profiling before shipping** — On-device latency, memory, and power measured on target hardware. Simulator numbers are rumors.
- **The cloud-vs-edge decision** — Updateability and scale vs latency, privacy, offline. A real tradeoff analysis — not a default.

